<document lang="en" type="paper">
  <chapter id="ch:regression-metrics">
    <title>Regression Metrics</title>
    <intro>
      These standalone functions compare observed targets with predictions.
      For one-dimensional inputs, each returns a scalar score. No estimator
      needs to be instantiated.
    </intro>
    <section id="metrics-notation">
      <title>Notation and inputs</title>
      Let $y=(y_1,\ldots,y_n)$ denote observations, $\hat y$ predictions and
      $\bar y=n^{-1}\sum_i y_i$ the mean of the supplied observations.
      The parameters y_true and y_pred accept numeric lists, tuples or NumPy
      arrays, converted to floating point. Supply nonempty, finite,
      one-dimensional arrays of equal length. The implementation checks
      conversion and length, but does not explicitly enforce dimensionality,
      nonemptiness or finiteness; do not rely on it to reject all invalid inputs.
      <c>total_sum_of_squares</c> takes only y_true. The additional parameter
      k in <c>r_squared_adjusted</c> is the number of features excluding the intercept.
    </section>
    <section id="metrics-imports">
      <title>Import</title>
      <code lang="python" numbered="false">
from ml_library.metrics.regression_metrics import (
    mean_squared_error,
    root_mean_squared_error,
    mean_absolute_error,
    mean_absolute_percentage_error,
    r_squared,
    r_squared_adjusted,
    residual_sum_of_squares,
    total_sum_of_squares,
)
      </code>
    </section>
    <section id="metrics-sums">
      <title>RSS and TSS</title>
      <c>residual_sum_of_squares(y_true, y_pred)</c> computes
      <equation>\mathrm{RSS}=\sum_{i=1}^n(y_i-\hat y_i)^2.</equation>
      This is the objective minimized by ordinary least squares.
      <c>total_sum_of_squares(y_true)</c> computes
      <equation>\mathrm{TSS}=\sum_{i=1}^n(y_i-\bar y)^2.</equation>
      TSS is the smallest RSS among constant predictions on the supplied
      observations and provides the reference for R squared.
    </section>
    <section id="metrics-squared-errors">
      <title>MSE and RMSE</title>
      <c>mean_squared_error(y_true, y_pred)</c> and
      <c>root_mean_squared_error(y_true, y_pred)</c> compute
      <equation>\mathrm{MSE}=\frac{\mathrm{RSS}}{n},\qquad
        \mathrm{RMSE}=\sqrt{\mathrm{MSE}}.</equation>
      Squaring gives large errors more weight, making these scores sensitive
      to outliers. MSE has squared target units; RMSE has the same units as
      the target. Smaller values indicate smaller prediction errors.
    </section>
    <section id="metrics-mae">
      <title>MAE</title>
      <c>mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred)</c> computes
      <equation>\mathrm{MAE}=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n|y_i-\hat y_i|.</equation>
      Absolute error penalizes deviations linearly and is less sensitive to
      extreme errors than squared error. MAE has the target's units, and
      any sample median minimizes the sum of absolute errors among constant predictions.
    </section>
    <section id="metrics-mape">
      <title>MAPE</title>
      <c>mean_absolute_percentage_error(y_true, y_pred)</c> computes
      <equation>\mathrm{MAPE}=\frac{100}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n
        \left|\frac{y_i-\hat y_i}{y_i}\right|.</equation>
      The result is a percentage, not a proportion. The function raises
      ValueError if any observed target is zero. Targets close to zero can
      dominate the score. Although negative nonzero targets are accepted,
      percentage interpretation is most meaningful when the target has a
      meaningful zero and relative errors are appropriate. MAPE can exceed 100 percent.
    </section>
    <section id="metrics-r2">
      <title>R squared</title>
      <c>r_squared(y_true, y_pred)</c> compares RSS with the constant mean reference:
      <equation>R^2=1-\frac{\mathrm{RSS}}{\mathrm{TSS}}.</equation>
      A perfect prediction has score one. Zero means the same RSS as the
      mean reference; a negative score means larger RSS. Thus this score is
      not restricted to the interval from zero to one. Its reference mean is
      computed from the supplied y_true, not from the training targets.
      A variance-explained interpretation is standard for an in-sample OLS fit
      with an intercept; for arbitrary predictions, read it as a relative
      squared-error score. Constant targets make TSS zero, and the function
      raises ValueError rather than returning a score.
    </section>
    <section id="metrics-adjusted-r2">
      <title>Adjusted R squared</title>
      <c>r_squared_adjusted(y_true, y_pred, k)</c> computes
      <equation>R^2_{\mathrm{adj}}=1-\frac{\mathrm{RSS}/(n-k-1)}{\mathrm{TSS}/(n-1)}
        =1-(1-R^2)\frac{n-1}{n-k-1}.</equation>
      The usual degrees-of-freedom interpretation concerns a full-rank linear
      model with k features and an intercept. Under the standard homoscedastic
      linear-model assumptions, RSS divided by $n-k-1$ estimates the error variance.
      Adding features cannot decrease training R squared for nested OLS fits
      on the same observations with an intercept. This statement does not
      apply to arbitrary models or to validation scores. Adjusted R squared
      penalizes the extra parameters and can decrease.
      The function requires a positive Python int for k and $n>k+1$.
      NumPy integer scalars such as np.int64 are rejected. Constant targets
      also raise ValueError. The formula is not a general complexity correction
      for regularized models or neural networks, and does not replace validation.
    </section>
    <section id="metrics-example">
      <title>Example</title>
      <code lang="python" numbered="false">
from ml_library.metrics.regression_metrics import (
    mean_squared_error, root_mean_squared_error, mean_absolute_error,
    mean_absolute_percentage_error, r_squared, r_squared_adjusted,
)

y_true = [3.0, -0.5, 2.0, 7.0]
y_pred = [2.5, 0.0, 2.0, 8.0]

print(mean_squared_error(y_true, y_pred))
print(root_mean_squared_error(y_true, y_pred))
print(mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred))
print(mean_absolute_percentage_error(y_true, y_pred))
print(r_squared(y_true, y_pred))
print(r_squared_adjusted(y_true, y_pred, k=1))
      </code>
      No single score fully describes performance. Inspect errors and evaluate
      on validation or test data: high training R squared does not establish
      good generalization. Compare models on the same observations and choose
      metrics that match the application.
    </section>
  </chapter>
</document>
